@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  A single microservice exposes one provided interface and
'                 requires one interface, wired to a client and a database.
'   usecase:      Teach component box notation and provided vs required
'                 interfaces (ball/lollipop vs socket/cup).
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       microservice / component diagram basics
' ============================================================================
title Example Component Diagram - Simple Microservice

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' STYLE: package-free layout so the reader sees raw components first.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
skinparam componentStyle uml2
skinparam shadowing false

' A COMPONENT is drawn as a box with two small tabs on top.
' Long form:  component "Display Name" as alias
' Short form: [Display Name]   (alias is auto-derived from the text)
component "Web Client" as client
component "Order Service" as svc
database "Orders DB" as db

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PROVIDED INTERFACE (ball / lollipop): the component OFFERS this contract.
' `() "Name" as id` declares a lollipop. `svc -()- IFoo` attaches it.
' A provided interface says: "callers may invoke this on me."
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
() "Submit Order" as iSubmit
svc -()- iSubmit

' The client CONSUMES the provided interface via a dependency arrow (..>).
' Dotted line = uses/depends-on (no compile-time binding required here).
client ..> iSubmit

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' REQUIRED INTERFACE (socket / cup): the component NEEDS this contract from
' someone else to do its job. `svc ..() IPersist` attaches a cup shape.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
() "Persist" as iPersist
svc ..() iPersist

' The database SATISFIES the required interface: it realizes (ball) what the
' service needs (cup). Drawing both ends shows the assembly forms a connection.
db -()- iPersist

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' LEGEND: a short note explaining the shapes shown in this diagram.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note bottom of svc
  **Provided** "Submit Order" = ball  (this service answers it)
  **Required** "Persist"      = cup   (this service needs it)
end note

@enduml
